# PLAN — Automated self-healing with a diagnostic memory

Build plan for the `rocket-fuel/self-heal` cycle. Vision and non-negotiables live in
`rocket-fuel/self-heal/VTO.md`; deferred items in `rocket-fuel/self-heal/DEFERRED.md`.

**Revision 3** — a restructure, not a diff. Same Page round 1 returned
`VERDICT: BLOCKED — 23 open finding(s)` (20 BLOCKER, 2 MAJOR, 3 KILL) and all 23 were
accepted; findings 1, 3, 13 and 18 each invalidated a load-bearing structural choice in
Revision 2, so patching was not available. Per-finding adjudication is in `SAME-PAGE-LOG.md`.
Revision 2 is preserved verbatim at `PLAN-rev2.md.bak`.

Revision 3 also adopts the operator-supplied architectural template (2026-09-14): a real
Python package with three deterministic guard layers and an orchestrator, type hints and
Google-style docstrings throughout, per-layer pytest coverage, config-driven thresholds, and
a README explaining why the guards live **outside** the thing they govern.

## The governing principle, restated

The template's central rule is *the guardrail must never rely on the governed system policing
itself; enforcement is deterministic, at the code layer.* Mapped onto this cycle:

> **The self-heal pipeline can never grant itself permission to run.** Every limit is
> evaluated by a separate component, from durable state the pipeline does not write during
> its own admission, before any work — including collection — begins. A repair process has no
> code path that can widen its own budget, reset its own breaker, or re-mint its own
> eligibility.

Revision 2 violated this in four places (findings 3, 4, 8, 16): it collected before gating, it
let a refusing CI step leave later steps eligible, it filtered self-origin incidents *after*
recording them, and its breaker reopened on a timer the pipeline's own inactivity satisfied.

## Package layout

A real package, replacing Revision 2's loose `scripts/*.py`:

```
selfheal/
  __init__.py
  config.py             # strict fail-closed runtime load  (finding 19)
  reasons.py            # reason-code constants, house pattern of scripts/reason_codes.py
  redaction.py          # recursive whole-object sanitization (finding 10)
  fingerprint.py        # keyed, sanitized-input-only identity (finding 11)
  admission_guard.py    # LAYER 1 — the gate, before any work   (findings 2,3,4,6,7,19)
  collection_guard.py   # LAYER 2 — bounded, provenance-filtered intake (4,5,14,15,16)
  publication_guard.py  # LAYER 3 — nothing unredacted/unverified escapes (10,11)
  ledger.py             # one authoritative sequenced store (2,17,21)
  adapters/             # incident -> repair contract (finding 13)
    __init__.py         #   registry; an incident class with no adapter is REPORT-ONLY
    base.py             #   Adapter protocol: matches(), repair(), verify()
    lint_format.py      #   the one adapter this cycle ships
  engine.py             # orchestration of the three layers
  cli.py                # `python -m selfheal` — --dry-run, --report, --reconcile
tests/selfheal/         # per-layer coverage, inside the root sweep
```

The three layers map 1:1 onto the template's input/retrieval/output guardrails:

| Template layer | Here | Enforces |
|---|---|---|
| Input guardrail — reject before any downstream call | `admission_guard` | May this run happen *at all*? Evaluated before collection, GitHub access, or any subprocess. |
| Retrieval guardrail — deterministic filtering at the code layer, never the model's discretion | `collection_guard` | What evidence may enter the ledger? Provenance-based self-origin exclusion, byte/incident caps, per-source deadlines. |
| Output guardrail — validate before anything reaches the user | `publication_guard` | What may leave the machine? Recursive redaction and a staged-bytes scan before any commit, PR body, or alert. |

## Starting point (facts, verified 2026-09-14 on this checkout)

Revision 2's fact list carries over except where round 1 corrected it. Corrections and
additions:

- **`scripts/self_heal.py` is broken today.** `step_3` runs `pytest --snapshot-update`, which
  this checkout rejects (`error: unrecognized arguments: --snapshot-update`); no snapshot
  plugin is installed. `run_cmd`'s `check=False` plus `except FileNotFoundError` swallow it,
  so the step has silently no-opped since it was written. `step_4`'s comment claims the stub
  installs are "Pinned, from the same file CI installs" — they carry no version specifiers.
  Repairing this baseline is Rock 6 scope, not an assumption (finding 12).
- **The secrets gate's exact patterns.** `tests/test_config_invariants.py:1266`
  `_ACCOUNT_SHAPED = re.compile(r"(?<![.\w])\d{6,12}(?![\w.])")`; line 1284 `_LABELED_ACCOUNT`
  matches `(?i)(?:account|acct|broker|robinhood|alpaca|rh)[^0-9A-Za-z]{0,3}(\d{6,12})`; line
  1475 `_SYNTHETIC_ACCOUNT_VALUES` exempts only `{FIXTURE_ACCOUNT_NUMBER, "123456789",
  "100000"}`. **No tracked config this cycle adds may contain a standalone 6–12 digit
  literal** (finding 9). `publication_guard` scans staged bytes with *both* patterns.
- **`scripts/tests` is inside the root sweep** — `testpaths = ["."]`, `addopts` names only the
  six sibling research trees, no `collect_ignore` (verified: 38 collected). New `selfheal/`
  code therefore falls under `[tool.coverage.run] source = ["."]` and the 90% gate
  automatically. Tests live at `tests/selfheal/`.
- **`jsonschema` 4.25.1 is installed** and
  `test_every_config_file_validates_against_its_referenced_schema` genuinely runs (not
  `importorskip`-skipped), so a `$schema` pointer earns real validation — at test time only,
  which is why finding 19's runtime validation is separate and required.
- **`gh` is present and authenticated** (`/opt/homebrew/bin/gh`, account `badMade`) — but
  **not on launchd's PATH**, which is finding 20.
- **The operator blanket-commits to `main`.** During this cycle `analysis/real-octopus-run/`
  went from untracked to 20 tracked files including `checkpoint/models/octopus/weights.npz`,
  and this cycle's own 436 KB Codex transcript was committed mid-review. No design may assume
  it controls what reaches a commit.

## Topology (finding 1 — the orphan branch is deleted)

Revision 2 pushed a digest to a `self-heal-diagnostics` orphan branch to trigger a privileged
workflow. Codex killed it: GitHub runs the workflow version associated with the event ref, so
a mutable ref would control a job holding `contents: write`. That is the same hole the file's
own header comment already argues against.

Revision 3:

```
  LOCAL MAC — the sole dispatcher                    GITHUB — ordinary, unprivileged
  ─────────────────────────────────                  ────────────────────────────────
  python -m selfheal  (launchd, daily)
    1. admission_guard.evaluate()   ← cheap gates ONLY; no I/O beyond durable state
         refuse ⇒ write reason to ledger, exit 0, do nothing else
    2. reserve()  ← atomic O_EXCL, keyed (run_id, fingerprint), worst-case cost debited
    3. collection_guard.collect()   ← bounded, deadline-enforced, provenance-filtered
    4. ledger.record() + select()   ← fair selection among ADAPTER-BACKED incidents only
    5. adapters[...].repair()       ← the incident is an INPUT (finding 13)
    6. adapters[...].verify()       ← source-specific success condition
    7. publication_guard.release()  ← recursive redaction + staged-bytes scan
    8. gh pr create  ─────────────────────────────▶  ordinary PR
    9. settle()  ← actuals replace the reservation      │
                                                        ▼
                                          the existing `CI` workflow validates it
                                          (no new trigger, no new permissions,
                                           no workflow on a mutable ref)
```

"CI healer" becomes **CI verifier** — the hosted sandbox still validates every repair, which
is what interview Q2 actually wanted, with nothing privileged reachable from a mutable ref.
`self-heal.yml`'s existing scheduled path keeps its own admission check as a separate gating
job (finding 8).

## Layer 1 — `selfheal/admission_guard.py`

`AdmissionGuard.evaluate(now, ledger, config, spend) -> Decision`, where
`Decision = (admit: bool, reason: str, reservation: Reservation | None)`. Two phases,
strictly ordered; **phase A performs no I/O beyond reading durable state** (finding 4):

**Phase A — cheap gates, before any work exists:**

| # | Gate | Config key | Default | Refusal reason |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | Kill switch | `enabled` / `$DCA_SELF_HEAL_DISABLED` | `true` / unset | `DISABLED` |
| 1 | Single-run lock | — | — | `ALREADY_RUNNING` |
| 2 | **Lifetime work bound** | `max_dispatches_per_rolling_window`, `rolling_window_days` | **7**, **30** | `LIFETIME_BOUND_REACHED` |
| 3 | **Latched breaker** | `consecutive_failure_threshold` | **3** | `BREAKER_LATCHED` |
| 4 | Daily rate limit | `max_runs_per_day` | **1** | `RATE_LIMITED_DAILY` |
| 5 | Clock sanity | `max_forward_skew_seconds` | **3600** | `CLOCK_IMPLAUSIBLE` |
| 6 | Actions-minute budget | `monthly_actions_minutes` | **300** | `BUDGET_EXHAUSTED_MINUTES` |

**Phase B — after bounded collection, before repair:**

| # | Gate | Config key | Default | Refusal reason |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 7 | Per-fingerprint attempts | `max_attempts_per_fingerprint` | **3** | `FINGERPRINT_QUARANTINED` |
| 8 | Adapter exists | — | — | `REPORT_ONLY_NO_ADAPTER` |
| 9 | Wall clock remaining | `max_wall_clock_seconds` | **1800** | `WALL_CLOCK_EXCEEDED` |
| 10 | Novelty | — | — | `NO_NOVEL_INCIDENTS` |

**Loop prevention, corrected (finding 3).** Revision 2's VTO claimed "any one mechanism alone
halts a runaway." That was **false** and is withdrawn. Daily buckets reset, fingerprints
re-mint, and a timed breaker reopens on the pipeline's own inactivity — those three throttle,
they do not halt. The two mechanisms that actually terminate are:

- **Gate 3, latched.** Three consecutive unsuccessful dispatches latch the breaker to `open`
  with **no automatic cooldown**. Only an explicit human `--reset-breaker` clears it. The
  pipeline has no code path that writes this field.
- **Gate 2, the lifetime bound.** At most 7 dispatches per rolling 30 days across *all*
  fingerprints, counted from the immutable ledger. This is the backstop that holds even if
  every other gate is misconfigured, and it is what makes "unbounded work" impossible rather
  than merely slow.

Gates 4, 7 and 8 remain as fairness and cost controls, not as the termination argument.

**Fail-closed.** Unparseable config, unreadable ledger, implausible clock, a lock it cannot
take, or any exception ⇒ `admit=False` with an explicit reason. No path where an error yields
permission. `--now` is accepted **only** under `--dry-run` or pytest (finding 7); live runs
use wall time, and all elapsed measurement uses `time.monotonic()`.

**Reservation (findings 2, 6).** On admit, an `O_EXCL` reservation file keyed
`(run_id, fingerprint)` is written *before* any repair work, debiting **worst-case** cost.
`settle()` replaces it with actuals; a reservation older than `max_wall_clock_seconds` with
no settlement is charged at worst case by the next run's reconciler, so a crash cannot refund
itself.

## Layer 2 — `selfheal/collection_guard.py`

`CollectionGuard.collect(deadline, caps) -> list[Incident]`. Five sources behind one
protocol, each with its own monotonic deadline, byte cap and incident cap; a source that
exceeds any of them is terminated by process group and recorded as a degraded-source incident
rather than silently dropped (finding 4).

**Durable evidence first (finding 5).** Before any fallible work, an immutable run envelope is
written to `$DCA_CRON_TRACKING_ROOT/self_heal/runs/<run_id>/envelope.json`. Complete
unredacted crash text is archived beside it. An independent emergency sink (`_emergency.log`,
opened `O_APPEND`) captures failures of the collector itself. Final status is written last;
an envelope with no final status is what the reconciler converts into a durable failure record.

**Provenance filtering happens before ledger insertion (finding 16).** Revision 2 recorded
first and filtered on path/branch/signature substrings, which miss a self-heal failure that
occurs on `main`. Revision 3 drops an incident when its **trusted** provenance says self-heal
caused it: the GitHub run id or workflow id is self-heal's, the PR number is one self-heal
opened (both read from the ledger, not guessed), or the causal parent run id is a self-heal
run. The substring check survives only as defence in depth.

Source specifics the round-1 review forced:

- `ci_runs` (finding 14): a persisted cursor, full pagination over every unseen run, and
  **failed-step log archival before the cursor advances**, so a burst or a collector outage
  cannot permanently hide a failure. `gh` absent or unauthenticated is a recorded degraded
  source, never fatal.
- `console_jobs` (finding 15): reads incrementally-persisted running state and bounded output,
  and treats a stale in-flight record as a durable failure — the hard-crash case, which
  selecting only nonzero terminal records missed entirely.
- `healthcheck`: each failing command is attributed as its own incident, under a per-command
  timeout.

## Layer 3 — `selfheal/publication_guard.py`

`PublicationGuard.release(obj, sink) -> bytes`. Nothing leaves the machine unexamined.

- **Recursive whole-object sanitization (finding 10).** `redaction.sanitize()` walks the entire
  object graph — every string in every nested dict, list and dataclass — not two named fields.
  Redaction happens in the `Incident` constructor, so an unredacted value is never reachable
  by a writer.
- **Staged-bytes scan.** Before any commit, PR body or alert, the **exact bytes to be
  published** are scanned with both `_ACCOUNT_SHAPED` and `_LABELED_ACCOUNT` from the
  repository's own gate, plus `broker123456789` / `rh_123456789`-style labelled forms. A hit
  aborts publication and latches the breaker.
- **Write-time containment.** Unredacted text may be written only under a path confirmed
  ignored by `git check-ignore`; the writer **refuses** rather than assuming
  `$DCA_CRON_TRACKING_ROOT` is ignored.
- **Keyed, sanitized-input fingerprints (finding 11).** `fingerprint.compute()` hashes only
  fully-sanitized canonical input, under an HMAC key stored beside `.lease_fingerprint_key`
  and never committed — so a published fingerprint is not an offline verification oracle for a
  low-entropy account number.

## `selfheal/adapters/` — the incident→repair contract (finding 13)

Revision 2 selected a fingerprint and then invoked a script that accepts no input: the
detector fed nothing into the healer, leaving operator requirement #2 unmet. Revision 3
defines:

```python
class Adapter(Protocol):
    def matches(self, incident: Incident) -> bool: ...
    def repair(self, incident: Incident, workspace: Path) -> RepairResult: ...
    def verify(self, incident: Incident, workspace: Path) -> VerifyResult: ...
```

`verify()` is **source-specific**: an adapter proves *that incident* is gone, not merely that
the suite is green. This cycle ships exactly one adapter — `lint_format`, wrapping the
`ruff check --fix` / `ruff format` steps that already work — and everything else is
**report-only by construction** (finding 18). The shard-1 MPS `NotImplementedError` is
collected and reported so the operator sees it, and is never dispatched, so it cannot consume
a daily slot or latch the breaker.

## `selfheal/ledger.py` (findings 2, 17, 21)

One authoritative sequenced store: append-only JSONL with a monotonic sequence number and a
per-record checksum, under `cross_process_lock`, with a torn final record truncated and
re-derived on load. `diagnostics/` mirrors and the compacted index are **derived** and fully
rebuildable from it; they are never read for an admission decision.

State machine, with Revision 2's off-by-one corrected — eligibility was `attempts < max` while
quarantine required `attempts > max`, so a fingerprint at exactly `max` was neither
dispatchable nor quarantined and silently vanished:

```
  new ──admit──▶ dispatched ──verify() passes & PR merged──▶ repaired
   ▲                  │
   │                  ├── verify() fails / PR closed / CI red ──▶ refused  (attempts += 1)
   │                  └── lease expired (crash, cancellation)  ──▶ refused  (attempts += 1)
   └──────────────────┘
  eligible ⟺ state ∈ {new, refused} ∧ attempts < max_attempts_per_fingerprint
  quarantined (terminal) ⟸ attempts ≥ max_attempts_per_fingerprint
```

`dispatched` carries a **lease**; the reconciler (`python -m selfheal --reconcile`, run at the
start of every cycle) resolves expired leases, closed PRs, cancellations and later merges.

## `config/self_heal.json` + `schemas/self_heal.schema.json`

Every threshold above lives here — raising the daily cap stays a one-line config edit, as the
operator asked. **No standalone 6–12 digit literal appears** (finding 9); the token/USD table
is deferred entirely, so the offending `200_000` is gone rather than exempted. Validation is
strict at **runtime** (finding 19), matching `dca_config`'s convention: duplicate keys,
non-object JSON, bools-as-ints, non-finite or negative values, missing fields, and missing
packaged config are each a refusal. The `$schema` pointer additionally earns the existing
test-time gate.

## Rocks

Seven. Per finding 22, **every** proof re-runs the existing suite, and the composition tests
exercise the crash, admission, public-commit and scheduler interleavings rather than each unit
in isolation.

Common suffix, abbreviated **[SUITE]** below:
`ruff check . && .venv/bin/python -m pytest -q && python run_dca.py --demo --report --now 2026-08-03T15:45 --artifacts-dir /tmp/dca-demo`

**Rock 1 — `config.py`, `reasons.py`, config + schema.** Strict fail-closed runtime load.
*Non-goals:* no gates, no I/O beyond reading the config.
*Proof:* `pytest tests/selfheal/test_config.py tests/test_config_invariants.py -q` — one
rejection test per corruption mode, plus a test asserting no tracked file this cycle adds
matches `_ACCOUNT_SHAPED` or `_LABELED_ACCOUNT` — then **[SUITE]**.

**Rock 2 — `redaction.py` + `fingerprint.py`.** Recursive sanitization; keyed,
sanitized-input-only identity.
*Non-goals:* no collection, no ledger.
*Proof:* `pytest tests/selfheal/test_redaction.py tests/selfheal/test_fingerprint.py -q` —
the real shard-1 MPS line fingerprints identically across differing timestamp, home path and
`shard_N`; a planted account number survives in **no** nested field of a recursively-walked
object; the fingerprint of a known account number differs under a rotated key (proving it is
not an unkeyed oracle) — then **[SUITE]**.

**Rock 3 — `admission_guard.py` + `ledger.py`.** All eleven gates, latched breaker, lifetime
bound, atomic reservation, sequenced store, lease reconciliation, corrected attempt boundary.
*Non-goals:* no collection, no repair.
*Proof:* `pytest tests/selfheal/test_admission_guard.py tests/selfheal/test_ledger.py -q` —
one test per gate in isolation; one fail-closed test per corruption mode; a **latch test**
proving no pipeline code path clears the breaker; a **lifetime-bound test** proving the 8th
dispatch in 30 days is refused however the other gates are configured; a boundary test at
exactly `attempts == max`; a torn-JSONL recovery test — then **[SUITE]**.

**Rock 4 — `collection_guard.py`.** Five sources, deadlines, caps, process-group termination,
run envelope, emergency sink, CI cursor + log archival, provenance filtering.
*Non-goals:* no repair, no publication.
*Proof:* `pytest tests/selfheal/test_collection_guard.py -q` — a source that hangs is killed
and recorded degraded; a killed collector still leaves a readable envelope and emergency-log
entry; a self-heal-caused failure **on `main`** is dropped by provenance before ledger
insertion; an unseen CI run beyond the first page is still collected and its log archived
before the cursor advances — then **[SUITE]**.

**Rock 5 — `publication_guard.py` + `adapters/`.** Staged-bytes scan, write-time containment,
the `lint_format` adapter, report-only fallback.
*Non-goals:* ships exactly one adapter; no others.
*Proof:* `pytest tests/selfheal/test_publication_guard.py tests/selfheal/test_adapters.py -q`
— a planted account number in a nested field **aborts** publication and latches the breaker;
every unredacted-bearing path written during a fixture run is confirmed by `git check-ignore`;
the MPS incident routes to report-only and consumes no slot; `lint_format.verify()` fails when
the specific incident persists even though the suite is green — then **[SUITE]**.

**Rock 6 — `engine.py`, `cli.py`, and repairing the broken baseline.** Orchestration; plus
finding 12: remove `--snapshot-update`, pin the stub installs, propagate errors, add
per-command timeouts.
*Non-goals:* no launchd, no workflow edit.
*Proof:* `pytest tests/selfheal/test_engine.py -q` — **composition tests**: a crash between
reserve and settle is charged at worst case by the next run; a refusal writes a reason and
performs no collection; two concurrent invocations yield exactly one reservation — plus
`python -m selfheal --dry-run --now 2026-09-14T04:00` printing a deterministic decision having
written nothing, and a test executing `scripts/self_heal.py`'s real commands — then **[SUITE]**.

**Rock 7 — launchd, workflow, docs, packaging, and two folded corrections.**
*Scope:* `deploy/launchd/render_self_heal_agent.py` with fully-resolved executables, explicit
no-`KeepAlive`, overlap prevention, durable stderr (finding 20); `self-heal.yml` admission as a
**separate job** gating every side-effecting job via `needs:`+`if:` (finding 8); `README.md`
for the package documenting the three layers and why the guard lives outside the governed
system; `docs/self_healing.md`; `pyproject.toml`; **Correction 1** — delete `workflow_dispatch:`
from `self-heal.yml`, keeping the header comment, which is the correct half; **Correction 2** —
fix CLAUDE.md's "Two suites sit outside the root `pytest` sweep" block, prose only, never
`addopts`.
*Proof:* `python scripts/workflow_policy.py`; `pytest tests/selfheal/test_workflow.py -q`
asserting a refused admission leaves every later job skipped; an acceptance check that the
agent **loads and kickstarts** and does not restart-loop — then **[SUITE]**.

## Open questions for round 2

- Does making the local Mac the sole dispatcher (finding 1's KILL) satisfy interview Q2's
  "CI healer", or has it quietly become a local healer with CI as a test runner? I believe the
  hosted sandbox still validating every repair is what mattered; Codex may disagree.
- Is the generic cost-unit reservation retained against finding 23's KILL still scope creep,
  given findings 2 and 6 require a reservation mechanism regardless?
- Does `max_dispatches_per_rolling_window = 7` interact badly with `max_runs_per_day = 1`
  — seven dispatches in thirty days is a 23-day dead zone after a bad week. Is a
  refusal-doesn't-count rule safe, or does it reintroduce the throttle-not-halt flaw?
- Shipping exactly one adapter means almost every real incident is report-only. Is that an
  honest MVP or a system that does not yet do what its name claims?
